{
  "dom_id": "step-42317",
  "name": "Likelihood vs Log-Likelihood",
  "problem": "The graph of the likelihood function \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14146425/assets/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14146425/assets/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point $\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}.$ This will become important later. \n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
